#! mathcas 2 notebook

#% title
# permutation_product

#% text
# `permutation_product(p1, p2, ...)`: the product, applying p1 first

#% section
# Details

#% item
# The product applies its arguments from left to right: the image of x under `permutation_product(p, q)` is the image under q of the image under p.

#% section
# Examples

#% subsection
# Basic examples

#% cell
permutation_product(cycles([[1, 2]]), cycles([[2, 3]]))
#% output
#= cycles([[1, 3, 2]])

#% cell
permutation_product(cycles([[2, 3]]), cycles([[1, 2]]))
#% output
#= cycles([[1, 2, 3]])

#% cell
permutation_product(cycles([[1, 2, 3]]), cycles([[1, 2, 3]]), cycles([[1, 2, 3]]))
#% output
#= cycles([])

#% section
# See also

#% text
# `permutation_inverse`, `permutation_power`, `cycles`
